/// Create a new mutex.
///
/// All newly-created mutexes begin in the *unlocked* state.
/// Calls to mutex.Mutex.lock() will not return while the mutex is locked by another thread. See mutex.Mutex.tryLock() to attempt to lock without blocking.
/// SDL mutexes are reentrant.
///
/// - **Returns:** the initialized and unlocked mutex or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** mutex.Mutex.deinit
/// - **See also:** mutex.Mutex.lock
/// - **See also:** mutex.Mutex.tryLock
/// - **See also:** mutex.Mutex.unlock
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createMutex() Error!Mutex {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateMutex();
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return Mutex{ .value = @ptrCast(result.?) };
}

/// Create a palette structure with the specified number of color entries.
///
/// The palette entries are initialized to white.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `ncolors`: represents the number of color entries in the color palette.
///
/// - **Returns:** a new pixels.Palette structure on success or NULL on failure (e.g. if there wasn't enough memory); call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** pixels.destroyPalette
/// - **See also:** pixels.setPaletteColors
/// - **See also:** surface.setPalette
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createPalette(ncolors: c_int) Error!*Palette {
    const result = c.SDL_CreatePalette(ncolors);
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return @ptrCast(result.?);
}

/// Create a child popup window of the specified parent window.
///
/// The window size is a request and may be different than expected based on the desktop layout and window manager policies. Your application should be prepared to handle a window of any size.
/// The flags parameter **must** contain at least one of the following:
/// - `video.WindowFlags.tooltip`: The popup window is a tooltip and will not pass any input events.
/// - `video.WindowFlags.popup_menu`: The popup window is a popup menu. The topmost popup menu will implicitly gain the keyboard focus.
/// The following flags are not relevant to popup window creation and will be ignored:
/// - `video.WindowFlags.minimized`
/// - `video.WindowFlags.maximized`
/// - `video.WindowFlags.fullscreen`
/// - `video.WindowFlags.borderless`
/// The following flags are incompatible with popup window creation and will cause it to fail:
/// - `video.WindowFlags.utility`
/// - `video.WindowFlags.modal`
/// The parent parameter **must** be non-null and a valid window. The parent of a popup window can be either a regular, toplevel window, or another popup window.
/// Popup windows cannot be minimized, maximized, made fullscreen, raised, flash, be made a modal window, be the parent of a toplevel window, or grab the mouse and/or keyboard. Attempts to do so will fail.
/// Popup windows implicitly do not have a border/decorations and do not appear on the taskbar/dock or in lists of windows such as alt-tab menus.
/// By default, popup window positions will automatically be constrained to keep the entire window within display bounds. This can be overridden with the `video.prop_window_create_constrain_popup_boolean` property.
/// By default, popup menus will automatically grab keyboard focus from the parent when shown. This behavior can be overridden by setting the `video.WindowFlags.not_focusable` flag, setting the `video.prop_window_create_focusable_boolean` property to false, or toggling it after creation via the `video.Window.setFocusable()` function.
/// If a parent window is hidden or destroyed, any child popup windows will be recursively hidden or destroyed as well. Child popup windows not explicitly hidden will be restored when the parent is shown.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `parent`: the parent of the window, must not be NULL.
///   - `offset_x`: the x position of the popup window relative to the origin of the parent.
///   - `offset_y`: the y position of the popup window relative to the origin of the parent window.
///   - `w`: the width of the window.
///   - `h`: the height of the window.
///   - `flags`: video.WindowFlags.tooltip or video.WindowFlags.popup_menu, and zero or more additional video.WindowFlags OR'd together.
///
/// - **Returns:** the window that was created or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should only be called on the main thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** video.createWindow
/// - **See also:** video.createWindowWithProperties
/// - **See also:** video.Window.deinit
/// - **See also:** video.Window.getParent
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createPopupWindow(parent: Window, offset_x: c_int, offset_y: c_int, w: c_int, h: c_int, flags: WindowFlags) Error!Window {
    const result = c.SDL_CreatePopupWindow(@ptrCast(parent.value), offset_x, offset_y, w, h, @bitCast(flags));
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return Window{ .value = @ptrCast(result.?) };
}

/// Create a new process.
///
/// The path to the executable is supplied in args[0]. args[1..N] are additional arguments passed on the command line of the new process, and the argument list should be terminated with a NULL, e.g.:
/// ```c
/// constchar*args[]={"myprogram","argument",NULL};
/// ```
///
/// Setting pipe_stdio to true is equivalent to setting `process.prop_process_create_stdin_number` and `process.prop_process_create_stdout_number` to `process.Io.app`, and will allow the use of process.read() or process.Process.getInput() and process.Process.getOutput().
/// See process.createWithProperties() for more details.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `args`: the path and arguments for the new process.
///   - `pipe_stdio`: true to create pipes to the process's standard input and from the process's standard output, false for the process to have no input and inherit the application's standard output.
///
/// - **Returns:** the newly created and running process, or NULL if the process couldn't be created.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** process.createWithProperties
/// - **See also:** process.Process.getProperties
/// - **See also:** process.read
/// - **See also:** process.Process.getInput
/// - **See also:** process.Process.getOutput
/// - **See also:** process.Process.kill
/// - **See also:** process.Process.wait
/// - **See also:** process.Process.deinit
inline fn createProcess(args: ?*const ?[*:0]const u8, pipe_stdio: bool) ?Process {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateProcess(@ptrCast(args), pipe_stdio);
    return if (result) |value| Process{ .value = @ptrCast(value) } else null;
}

/// Create a new process with the specified properties.
///
/// These are the supported properties:
/// - `process.prop_process_create_args_pointer`: an array of strings containing the program to run, any arguments, and a NULL pointer, e.g. const char *args[] = { "myprogram", "argument", NULL }. This is a required property.
/// - `process.prop_process_create_environment_pointer`: an stdinc.Environment pointer. If this property is set, it will be the entire environment for the process, otherwise the current environment is used.
/// - `process.prop_process_create_working_directory_string`: a UTF-8 encoded string representing the working directory for the process, defaults to the current working directory.
/// - `process.prop_process_create_stdin_number`: an process.Io value describing where standard input for the process comes from, defaults to `process.Io.null_`.
/// - `process.prop_process_create_stdin_pointer`: an ioStream.IoStream pointer used for standard input when `process.prop_process_create_stdin_number` is set to `process.Io.redirect`.
/// - `process.prop_process_create_stdout_number`: an process.Io value describing where standard output for the process goes to, defaults to `process.Io.inherited`.
/// - `process.prop_process_create_stdout_pointer`: an ioStream.IoStream pointer used for standard output when `process.prop_process_create_stdout_number` is set to `process.Io.redirect`.
/// - `process.prop_process_create_stderr_number`: an process.Io value describing where standard error for the process goes to, defaults to `process.Io.inherited`.
/// - `process.prop_process_create_stderr_pointer`: an ioStream.IoStream pointer used for standard error when `process.prop_process_create_stderr_number` is set to `process.Io.redirect`.
/// - `process.prop_process_create_stderr_to_stdout_boolean`: true if the error output of the process should be redirected into the standard output of the process. This property has no effect if `process.prop_process_create_stderr_number` is set.
/// - `process.prop_process_create_background_boolean`: true if the process should run in the background. In this case the default input and output is `process.Io.null_` and the exitcode of the process is not available, and will always be 0.
/// - `process.prop_process_create_cmdline_string`: a string containing the program to run and any parameters. This string is passed directly to `CreateProcess` on Windows, and does nothing on other platforms. This property is only important if you want to start programs that does non-standard command-line processing, and in most cases using `process.prop_process_create_args_pointer` is sufficient.
/// On POSIX platforms, wait() and waitpid(-1, ...) should not be called, and SIGCHLD should not be ignored or handled because those would prevent SDL from properly tracking the lifetime of the underlying process. You should use process.Process.wait() instead.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `props`: the properties to use.
///
/// - **Returns:** the newly created and running process, or NULL if the process couldn't be created.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** process.create
/// - **See also:** process.Process.getProperties
/// - **See also:** process.read
/// - **See also:** process.Process.getInput
/// - **See also:** process.Process.getOutput
/// - **See also:** process.Process.kill
/// - **See also:** process.Process.wait
/// - **See also:** process.Process.deinit
inline fn createProcessWithProperties(props: PropertiesId) ?Process {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateProcessWithProperties(props);
    return if (result) |value| Process{ .value = @ptrCast(value) } else null;
}

/// Create a group of properties.
///
/// All properties are automatically destroyed when quit() is called.
///
/// - **Returns:** an ID for a new group of properties, or 0 on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** properties.destroy
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createProperties() Error!PropertiesId {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateProperties();
    if (result == 0) return error.SdlFailure;
    return result;
}

/// Create a 2D rendering context for a window.
///
/// If you want a specific renderer, you can specify its name here. A list of available renderers can be obtained by calling render.getDriver() multiple times, with indices from 0 to render.getNumDrivers()-1. If you don't need a specific renderer, specify NULL and SDL will attempt to choose the best option for you, based on what is available on the user's system.
/// If `name` is a comma-separated list, SDL will try each name, in the order listed, until one succeeds or all of them fail.
/// By default the rendering size matches the window size in pixels, but you can call render.Renderer.setRenderLogicalPresentation() to change the content size and scaling options.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `window`: the window where rendering is displayed.
///   - `name`: the name of the rendering driver to initialize, or NULL to let SDL choose one.
///
/// - **Returns:** a valid rendering context or NULL if there was an error; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should only be called on the main thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** render.createRendererWithProperties
/// - **See also:** render.createSoftwareRenderer
/// - **See also:** render.Renderer.deinit
/// - **See also:** render.getNumDrivers
/// - **See also:** render.getDriver
/// - **See also:** render.Renderer.getName
inline fn createRenderer(window: ?Window, name: ?[:0]const u8) ?Renderer {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateRenderer(if (window) |resource| @ptrCast(resource.value) else null, if (name != null) @ptrCast(name.?.ptr) else null);
    return if (result) |value| Renderer{ .value = @ptrCast(value) } else null;
}

/// Create a 2D rendering context for a window, with the specified properties.
///
/// These are the supported properties:
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_name_string`: the name of the rendering driver to use, if a specific one is desired
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_window_pointer`: the window where rendering is displayed, required if this isn't a software renderer using a surface
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_surface_pointer`: the surface where rendering is displayed, if you want a software renderer without a window
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_output_colorspace_number`: an pixels.Colorspace value describing the colorspace for output to the display, defaults to pixels.Colorspace.srgb. The direct3d11, direct3d12, and metal renderers support pixels.Colorspace.srgb_linear, which is a linear color space and supports HDR output. If you select pixels.Colorspace.srgb_linear, drawing still uses the sRGB colorspace, but values can go beyond 1.0 and float (linear) format textures can be used for HDR content.
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_present_vsync_number`: non-zero if you want present synchronized with the refresh rate. This property can take any value that is supported by render.Renderer.setRenderVSync() for the renderer.
/// With the SDL GPU renderer (since SDL 3.4.0):
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_gpu_device_pointer`: the device to use with the renderer, optional.
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_gpu_shaders_spirv_boolean`: the app is able to provide SPIR-V shaders to render.GpuState, optional.
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_gpu_shaders_dxil_boolean`: the app is able to provide DXIL shaders to render.GpuState, optional.
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_gpu_shaders_msl_boolean`: the app is able to provide MSL shaders to render.GpuState, optional.
/// With the vulkan renderer:
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_vulkan_instance_pointer`: the VkInstance to use with the renderer, optional.
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_vulkan_surface_number`: the VkSurfaceKHR to use with the renderer, optional.
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_vulkan_physical_device_pointer`: the VkPhysicalDevice to use with the renderer, optional.
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_vulkan_device_pointer`: the VkDevice to use with the renderer, optional.
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_vulkan_graphics_queue_family_index_number`: the queue family index used for rendering.
/// - `render.prop_renderer_create_vulkan_present_queue_family_index_number`: the queue family index used for presentation.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `props`: the properties to use.
///
/// - **Returns:** a valid rendering context or NULL if there was an error; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should only be called on the main thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** properties.create
/// - **See also:** render.createRenderer
/// - **See also:** render.createSoftwareRenderer
/// - **See also:** render.Renderer.deinit
/// - **See also:** render.Renderer.getName
inline fn createRendererWithProperties(props: PropertiesId) ?Renderer {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateRendererWithProperties(props);
    return if (result) |value| Renderer{ .value = @ptrCast(value) } else null;
}

/// Create a new read/write lock.
///
/// A read/write lock is useful for situations where you have multiple threads trying to access a resource that is rarely updated. All threads requesting a read-only lock will be allowed to run in parallel; if a thread requests a write lock, it will be provided exclusive access. This makes it safe for multiple threads to use a resource at the same time if they promise not to change it, and when it has to be changed, the rwlock will serve as a gateway to make sure those changes can be made safely.
/// In the right situation, a rwlock can be more efficient than a mutex, which only lets a single thread proceed at a time, even if it won't be modifying the data.
/// All newly-created read/write locks begin in the *unlocked* state.
/// Calls to mutex.RwLock.lockForReading() and mutex.RwLock.lockForWriting will not return while the rwlock is locked *for writing* by another thread. See mutex.RwLock.tryLockForReading() and mutex.RwLock.tryLockForWriting() to attempt to lock without blocking.
/// SDL read/write locks are only recursive for read-only locks! They are not guaranteed to be fair, or provide access in a FIFO manner! They are not guaranteed to favor writers. You may not lock a rwlock for both read-only and write access at the same time from the same thread (so you can't promote your read-only lock to a write lock without unlocking first).
///
/// - **Returns:** the initialized and unlocked read/write lock or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** mutex.RwLock.deinit
/// - **See also:** mutex.RwLock.lockForReading
/// - **See also:** mutex.RwLock.lockForWriting
/// - **See also:** mutex.RwLock.tryLockForReading
/// - **See also:** mutex.RwLock.tryLockForWriting
/// - **See also:** mutex.RwLock.unlock
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createRwLock() Error!RwLock {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateRWLock();
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return RwLock{ .value = @ptrCast(result.?) };
}

/// Create a semaphore.
///
/// This function creates a new semaphore and initializes it with the value `initial_value`. Each wait operation on the semaphore will atomically decrement the semaphore value and potentially block if the semaphore value is 0. Each post operation will atomically increment the semaphore value and wake waiting threads and allow them to retry the wait operation.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `initial_value`: the starting value of the semaphore.
///
/// - **Returns:** a new semaphore or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** mutex.Semaphore.deinit
/// - **See also:** mutex.Semaphore.signal
/// - **See also:** mutex.Semaphore.tryWait
/// - **See also:** mutex.Semaphore.getValue
/// - **See also:** mutex.Semaphore.wait
/// - **See also:** mutex.Semaphore.waitTimeout
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createSemaphore(initial_value: u32) Error!Semaphore {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateSemaphore(initial_value);
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return Semaphore{ .value = @ptrCast(result.?) };
}

/// Create a 2D software rendering context for a surface.
///
/// Two other API which can be used to create render.Renderer: render.createRenderer() and render.createWindowAndRenderer(). These can *also* create a software renderer, but they are intended to be used with an video.Window as the final destination and not an surface.Surface.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `surface_2`: the surface.Surface structure representing the surface where rendering is done.
///
/// - **Returns:** a valid rendering context or NULL if there was an error; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** render.Renderer.deinit
inline fn createSoftwareRenderer(surface_2: ?*Surface) ?Renderer {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateSoftwareRenderer(@ptrCast(surface_2));
    return if (result) |value| Renderer{ .value = @ptrCast(value) } else null;
}

/// Create a directory in a writable storage container.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `storage_2`: a storage container.
///   - `path`: the path of the directory to create.
///
/// - **Returns:** true on success or false on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** storage.Storage.ready
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createStorageDirectory(storage_2: ?Storage, path: ?[:0]const u8) Error!void {
    if (!c.SDL_CreateStorageDirectory(if (storage_2) |resource| @ptrCast(resource.value) else null, if (path != null) @ptrCast(path.?.ptr) else null)) return error.SdlFailure;
}

/// Allocate a new surface with a specific pixel format.
///
/// The pixels of the new surface are initialized to zero.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `width`: the width of the surface.
///   - `height`: the height of the surface.
///   - `format`: the pixels.PixelFormat for the new surface's pixel format.
///
/// - **Returns:** the new surface.Surface structure that is created or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** surface.createFrom
/// - **See also:** surface.destroy
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createSurface(width: c_int, height: c_int, format: PixelFormat) Error!*Surface {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateSurface(width, height, @intCast(@intFromEnum(format)));
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return @ptrCast(result.?);
}

/// Allocate a new surface with a specific pixel format and existing pixel data.
///
/// No copy is made of the pixel data. Pixel data is not managed automatically; you must free the surface before you free the pixel data.
/// Pitch is the offset in bytes from one row of pixels to the next, e.g. `width*4` for `pixels.PixelFormat.rgba8888`.
/// You may pass NULL for pixels and 0 for pitch to create a surface that you will fill in with valid values later.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `width`: the width of the surface.
///   - `height`: the height of the surface.
///   - `format`: the pixels.PixelFormat for the new surface's pixel format.
///   - `pixels_2`: a pointer to existing pixel data.
///   - `pitch`: the number of bytes between each row, including padding.
///
/// - **Returns:** the new surface.Surface structure that is created or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** surface.create
/// - **See also:** surface.destroy
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createSurfaceFrom(width: c_int, height: c_int, format: PixelFormat, pixels_2: ?*anyopaque, pitch: c_int) Error!*Surface {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateSurfaceFrom(width, height, @intCast(@intFromEnum(format)), @ptrCast(pixels_2), pitch);
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return @ptrCast(result.?);
}

/// Create a palette and associate it with a surface.
///
/// This function creates a palette compatible with the provided surface. The palette is then returned for you to modify, and the surface will automatically use the new palette in future operations. You do not need to destroy the returned palette, it will be freed when the reference count reaches 0, usually when the surface is destroyed.
/// Bitmap surfaces (with format pixels.PixelFormat.index1_lsb or pixels.PixelFormat.index1_msb) will have the palette initialized with 0 as white and 1 as black. Other surfaces will get a palette initialized with white in every entry.
/// If this function is called for a surface that already has a palette, a new palette will be created to replace it.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `surface_2`: the surface.Surface structure to update.
///
/// - **Returns:** a new pixels.Palette structure on success or NULL on failure (e.g. if the surface didn't have an index format); call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function can be called on different threads with different surfaces.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** pixels.setPaletteColors
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createSurfacePalette(surface_2: ?*Surface) Error!*Palette {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateSurfacePalette(@ptrCast(surface_2));
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return @ptrCast(result.?);
}

/// Create a system cursor.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `id`: an mouse.SystemCursor enum value.
///
/// - **Returns:** a cursor on success or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should only be called on the main thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** mouse.Cursor.deinit
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createSystemCursor(id: SystemCursor) Error!Cursor {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateSystemCursor(@intCast(@intFromEnum(id)));
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return Cursor{ .value = @ptrCast(result.?) };
}

/// Create a texture for a rendering context.
///
/// The contents of a texture when first created are not defined.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `renderer`: the rendering context.
///   - `format`: one of the enumerated values in pixels.PixelFormat.
///   - `access`: one of the enumerated values in render.TextureAccess.
///   - `w`: the width of the texture in pixels.
///   - `h`: the height of the texture in pixels.
///
/// - **Returns:** the created texture or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should only be called on the main thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** render.Renderer.createTextureFromSurface
/// - **See also:** render.Renderer.createTextureWithProperties
/// - **See also:** render.destroyTexture
/// - **See also:** render.getTextureSize
/// - **See also:** render.updateTexture
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createTexture(renderer: ?Renderer, format: PixelFormat, access: TextureAccess, w: c_int, h: c_int) Error!*Texture {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateTexture(if (renderer) |resource| @ptrCast(resource.value) else null, @intCast(@intFromEnum(format)), @intCast(@intFromEnum(access)), w, h);
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return @ptrCast(result.?);
}

/// Create a texture from an existing surface.
///
/// The surface is not modified or freed by this function.
/// The render.TextureAccess hint for the created texture is `render.TextureAccess.static`.
/// The pixel format of the created texture may be different from the pixel format of the surface, and can be queried using the render.prop_texture_format_number property.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `renderer`: the rendering context.
///   - `surface_2`: the surface.Surface structure containing pixel data used to fill the texture.
///
/// - **Returns:** the created texture or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should only be called on the main thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** render.Renderer.createTexture
/// - **See also:** render.Renderer.createTextureWithProperties
/// - **See also:** render.destroyTexture
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createTextureFromSurface(renderer: ?Renderer, surface_2: ?*Surface) Error!*Texture {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateTextureFromSurface(if (renderer) |resource| @ptrCast(resource.value) else null, @ptrCast(surface_2));
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return @ptrCast(result.?);
}

/// Create a texture for a rendering context with the specified properties.
///
/// These are the supported properties:
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_colorspace_number`: an pixels.Colorspace value describing the texture colorspace, defaults to pixels.Colorspace.srgb_linear for floating point textures, pixels.Colorspace.hdr10 for 10-bit textures, pixels.Colorspace.srgb for other RGB textures and pixels.Colorspace.jpeg for YUV textures.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_format_number`: one of the enumerated values in pixels.PixelFormat, defaults to the best RGBA format for the renderer
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_access_number`: one of the enumerated values in render.TextureAccess, defaults to render.TextureAccess.static
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_width_number`: the width of the texture in pixels, required
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_height_number`: the height of the texture in pixels, required
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_palette_pointer`: an pixels.Palette to use with palettized texture formats. This can be set later with render.setTexturePalette()
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_sdr_white_point_float`: for HDR10 and floating point textures, this defines the value of 100% diffuse white, with higher values being displayed in the High Dynamic Range headroom. This defaults to 100 for HDR10 textures and 1.0 for floating point textures.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_hdr_headroom_float`: for HDR10 and floating point textures, this defines the maximum dynamic range used by the content, in terms of the SDR white point. This would be equivalent to maxCLL / render.prop_texture_create_sdr_white_point_float for HDR10 content. If this is defined, any values outside the range supported by the display will be scaled into the available HDR headroom, otherwise they are clipped.
/// With the direct3d11 renderer:
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_d3d11_texture_pointer`: the ID3D11Texture2D associated with the texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_d3d11_texture_u_pointer`: the ID3D11Texture2D associated with the U plane of a YUV texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_d3d11_texture_v_pointer`: the ID3D11Texture2D associated with the V plane of a YUV texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// With the direct3d12 renderer:
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_d3d12_texture_pointer`: the ID3D12Resource associated with the texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_d3d12_texture_u_pointer`: the ID3D12Resource associated with the U plane of a YUV texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_d3d12_texture_v_pointer`: the ID3D12Resource associated with the V plane of a YUV texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// With the metal renderer:
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_metal_pixel_buffer_pointer`: the CVPixelBufferRef associated with the texture, if you want to create a texture from an existing pixel buffer.
/// With the opengl renderer:
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_open_gl_texture_number`: the GLuint texture associated with the texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_open_gl_texture_uv_number`: the GLuint texture associated with the UV plane of an NV12 texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_open_gl_texture_u_number`: the GLuint texture associated with the U plane of a YUV texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_open_gl_texture_v_number`: the GLuint texture associated with the V plane of a YUV texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// With the opengles2 renderer:
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_opengles2_texture_number`: the GLuint texture associated with the texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_opengles2_texture_uv_number`: the GLuint texture associated with the UV plane of an NV12 texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_opengles2_texture_u_number`: the GLuint texture associated with the U plane of a YUV texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_opengles2_texture_v_number`: the GLuint texture associated with the V plane of a YUV texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// With the vulkan renderer:
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_vulkan_texture_number`: the VkImage associated with the texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_vulkan_layout_number`: the VkImageLayout for the VkImage, defaults to VK_IMAGE_LAYOUT_SHADER_READ_ONLY_OPTIMAL.
/// With the GPU renderer:
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_gpu_texture_pointer`: the gpu.Texture associated with the texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_gpu_texture_uv_pointer`: the gpu.Texture associated with the UV plane of an NV12 texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_gpu_texture_u_pointer`: the gpu.Texture associated with the U plane of a YUV texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
/// - `render.prop_texture_create_gpu_texture_v_pointer`: the gpu.Texture associated with the V plane of a YUV texture, if you want to wrap an existing texture.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `renderer`: the rendering context.
///   - `props`: the properties to use.
///
/// - **Returns:** the created texture or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should only be called on the main thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** properties.create
/// - **See also:** render.Renderer.createTexture
/// - **See also:** render.Renderer.createTextureFromSurface
/// - **See also:** render.destroyTexture
/// - **See also:** render.getTextureSize
/// - **See also:** render.updateTexture
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createTextureWithProperties(renderer: ?Renderer, props: PropertiesId) Error!*Texture {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateTextureWithProperties(if (renderer) |resource| @ptrCast(resource.value) else null, props);
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return @ptrCast(result.?);
}

/// The actual entry point for SDL_CreateThread (C macro).
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `fn_`: the thread.Function function to call in the new thread
///   - `name`: the name of the thread
///   - `data`: a pointer that is passed to `fn_`
///   - `pfn_begin_thread`: the C runtime's _beginthreadex (or whatnot). Can be NULL.
///   - `pfn_end_thread`: the C runtime's _endthreadex (or whatnot). Can be NULL.
///
/// - **Returns:** an opaque pointer to the new thread object on success, NULL if the new thread could not be created; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
inline fn createThreadRuntime(fn_: ThreadFunction, name: ?[:0]const u8, data: ?*anyopaque, pfn_begin_thread: FunctionPointer, pfn_end_thread: FunctionPointer) ?Thread {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateThreadRuntime(@ptrCast(fn_), if (name != null) @ptrCast(name.?.ptr) else null, @ptrCast(data), @ptrCast(pfn_begin_thread), @ptrCast(pfn_end_thread));
    return if (result) |value| Thread{ .value = @ptrCast(value) } else null;
}

/// The actual entry point for SDL_CreateThreadWithProperties (C macro).
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `props`: the properties to use
///   - `pfn_begin_thread`: the C runtime's _beginthreadex (or whatnot). Can be NULL.
///   - `pfn_end_thread`: the C runtime's _endthreadex (or whatnot). Can be NULL.
///
/// - **Returns:** an opaque pointer to the new thread object on success, NULL if the new thread could not be created; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
inline fn createThreadWithPropertiesRuntime(props: PropertiesId, pfn_begin_thread: FunctionPointer, pfn_end_thread: FunctionPointer) ?Thread {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateThreadWithPropertiesRuntime(props, @ptrCast(pfn_begin_thread), @ptrCast(pfn_end_thread));
    return if (result) |value| Thread{ .value = @ptrCast(value) } else null;
}

/// Create an icon to be placed in the operating system's tray, or equivalent.
///
/// Many platforms advise not using a system tray unless persistence is a necessary feature. Avoid needlessly creating a tray icon, as the user may feel like it clutters their interface.
/// Using tray icons require the video subsystem.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `icon`: a surface to be used as icon. May be NULL.
///   - `tooltip`: a tooltip to be displayed when the mouse hovers the icon in UTF-8 encoding. Not supported on all platforms. May be NULL.
///
/// - **Returns:** The newly created system tray icon.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should only be called on the main thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** tray.Tray.createMenu
/// - **See also:** tray.Tray.getMenu
/// - **See also:** tray.Tray.deinit
inline fn createTray(icon: ?*Surface, tooltip: ?[:0]const u8) ?Tray {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateTray(@ptrCast(icon), if (tooltip != null) @ptrCast(tooltip.?.ptr) else null);
    return if (result) |value| Tray{ .value = @ptrCast(value) } else null;
}

/// Create a menu for a system tray.
///
/// This should be called at most once per tray icon.
/// This function does the same thing as tray.createSubmenu(), except that it takes a tray.Tray instead of a tray.Entry.
/// A menu does not need to be destroyed; it will be destroyed with the tray.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `tray_2`: the tray to bind the menu to.
///
/// - **Returns:** the newly created menu.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should be called on the thread that created the tray.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** tray.create
/// - **See also:** tray.Tray.getMenu
/// - **See also:** tray.getMenuParentTray
inline fn createTrayMenu(tray_2: ?Tray) ?*TrayMenu {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateTrayMenu(if (tray_2) |resource| @ptrCast(resource.value) else null);
    return if (result == null) null else @ptrCast(result);
}

/// Create a submenu for a system tray entry.
///
/// This should be called at most once per tray entry.
/// This function does the same thing as tray.Tray.createMenu, except that it takes a tray.Entry instead of a tray.Tray.
/// A menu does not need to be destroyed; it will be destroyed with the tray.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `entry`: the tray entry to bind the menu to.
///
/// - **Returns:** the newly created menu.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should be called on the thread that created the tray.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** tray.insertEntryAt
/// - **See also:** tray.getSubmenu
/// - **See also:** tray.getMenuParentEntry
inline fn createTraySubmenu(entry: ?*TrayEntry) ?*TrayMenu {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateTraySubmenu(@ptrCast(entry));
    return if (result == null) null else @ptrCast(result);
}

/// Create a window with the specified dimensions and flags.
///
/// The window size is a request and may be different than expected based on the desktop layout and window manager policies. Your application should be prepared to handle a window of any size.
/// `flags` may be any of the following OR'd together:
/// - `video.WindowFlags.fullscreen`: fullscreen window at desktop resolution
/// - `video.WindowFlags.open_gl`: window usable with an OpenGL context
/// - `video.WindowFlags.hidden`: window is not visible
/// - `video.WindowFlags.borderless`: no window decoration
/// - `video.WindowFlags.resizable`: window can be resized
/// - `video.WindowFlags.minimized`: window is minimized
/// - `video.WindowFlags.maximized`: window is maximized
/// - `video.WindowFlags.mouse_grabbed`: window has grabbed mouse focus
/// - `video.WindowFlags.input_focus`: window has input focus
/// - `video.WindowFlags.mouse_focus`: window has mouse focus
/// - `video.WindowFlags.external`: window not created by SDL
/// - `video.WindowFlags.modal`: window is modal
/// - `video.WindowFlags.high_pixel_density`: window uses high pixel density back buffer if possible
/// - `video.WindowFlags.mouse_capture`: window has mouse captured (unrelated to MOUSE_GRABBED)
/// - `video.WindowFlags.always_on_top`: window should always be above others
/// - `video.WindowFlags.utility`: window should be treated as a utility window, not showing in the task bar and window list
/// - `video.WindowFlags.tooltip`: window should be treated as a tooltip and does not get mouse or keyboard focus, requires a parent window
/// - `video.WindowFlags.popup_menu`: window should be treated as a popup menu, requires a parent window
/// - `video.WindowFlags.keyboard_grabbed`: window has grabbed keyboard input
/// - `video.WindowFlags.vulkan`: window usable with a Vulkan instance
/// - `video.WindowFlags.metal`: window usable with a Metal instance
/// - `video.WindowFlags.transparent`: window with transparent buffer
/// - `video.WindowFlags.not_focusable`: window should not be focusable
/// The video.Window will be shown if video.WindowFlags.hidden is not set. If hidden at creation time, video.Window.show() can be used to show it later.
/// On Apple's macOS, you **must** set the NSHighResolutionCapable Info.plist property to YES, otherwise you will not receive a High-DPI OpenGL canvas.
/// The window pixel size may differ from its window coordinate size if the window is on a high pixel density display. Use video.Window.getSize() to query the client area's size in window coordinates, and video.Window.getSizeInPixels() or render.Renderer.getRenderOutputSize() to query the drawable size in pixels. Note that the drawable size can vary after the window is created and should be queried again if you get an events.EventType.window_pixel_size_changed event.
/// If the window is created with any of the video.WindowFlags.open_gl or video.WindowFlags.vulkan flags, then the corresponding LoadLibrary function (video.glLoadLibrary or SDL_Vulkan_LoadLibrary (C API outside this module)) is called and the corresponding UnloadLibrary function is called by video.Window.deinit().
/// If video.WindowFlags.vulkan is specified and there isn't a working Vulkan driver, video.createWindow() will fail, because SDL_Vulkan_LoadLibrary (C API outside this module)() will fail.
/// If video.WindowFlags.metal is specified on an OS that does not support Metal, video.createWindow() will fail.
/// If you intend to use this window with an render.Renderer, you should use render.createWindowAndRenderer() instead of this function, to avoid window flicker.
/// On non-Apple devices, SDL requires you to either not link to the Vulkan loader or link to a dynamic library version. This limitation may be removed in a future version of SDL.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `title`: the title of the window, in UTF-8 encoding.
///   - `w`: the width of the window.
///   - `h`: the height of the window.
///   - `flags`: 0, or one or more video.WindowFlags OR'd together.
///
/// - **Returns:** the window that was created or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should only be called on the main thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** render.createWindowAndRenderer
/// - **See also:** video.createPopupWindow
/// - **See also:** video.createWindowWithProperties
/// - **See also:** video.Window.deinit
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createWindow(title: ?[:0]const u8, w: c_int, h: c_int, flags: WindowFlags) Error!Window {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateWindow(if (title != null) @ptrCast(title.?.ptr) else null, w, h, @bitCast(flags));
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return Window{ .value = @ptrCast(result.?) };
}

/// Named output values.
const CreateWindowAndRendererResult = struct {
    /// Output `window`.
    window: Window,
    /// Output `renderer`.
    renderer: Renderer,
};

/// Create a window and default renderer.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `title`: the title of the window, in UTF-8 encoding.
///   - `width`: the width of the window.
///   - `height`: the height of the window.
///   - `window_flags`: the flags used to create the window (see video.createWindow()).
///
/// - **Returns:** true on success or false on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should only be called on the main thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** render.createRenderer
/// - **See also:** video.createWindow
/// Returns named output values.
inline fn createWindowAndRenderer(title: ?[:0]const u8, width: c_int, height: c_int, window_flags: WindowFlags) Error!CreateWindowAndRendererResult {
    var window_raw: ?*c.SDL_Window = null;
    var renderer_raw: ?*c.SDL_Renderer = null;
    if (!c.SDL_CreateWindowAndRenderer(if (title != null) @ptrCast(title.?.ptr) else null, width, height, @bitCast(window_flags), &window_raw, &renderer_raw)) return error.SdlFailure;
    return CreateWindowAndRendererResult{
        .window = Window{ .value = @ptrCast(window_raw orelse return error.SdlFailure) },
        .renderer = Renderer{ .value = @ptrCast(renderer_raw orelse return error.SdlFailure) },
    };
}

/// Create a window with the specified properties.
///
/// The window size is a request and may be different than expected based on the desktop layout and window manager policies. Your application should be prepared to handle a window of any size.
/// These are the supported properties:
/// - `video.prop_window_create_always_on_top_boolean`: true if the window should be always on top
/// - `video.prop_window_create_borderless_boolean`: true if the window has no window decoration
/// - `video.prop_window_create_constrain_popup_boolean`: true if the "tooltip" and "menu" window types should be automatically constrained to be entirely within display bounds (default), false if no constraints on the position are desired.
/// - `video.prop_window_create_external_graphics_context_boolean`: true if the window will be used with an externally managed graphics context.
/// - `video.prop_window_create_focusable_boolean`: true if the window should accept keyboard input (defaults true)
/// - `video.prop_window_create_fullscreen_boolean`: true if the window should start in fullscreen mode at desktop resolution
/// - `video.prop_window_create_height_number`: the height of the window
/// - `video.prop_window_create_hidden_boolean`: true if the window should start hidden
/// - `video.prop_window_create_high_pixel_density_boolean`: true if the window uses a high pixel density buffer if possible
/// - `video.prop_window_create_maximized_boolean`: true if the window should start maximized
/// - `video.prop_window_create_menu_boolean`: true if the window is a popup menu
/// - `video.prop_window_create_metal_boolean`: true if the window will be used with Metal rendering
/// - `video.prop_window_create_minimized_boolean`: true if the window should start minimized
/// - `video.prop_window_create_modal_boolean`: true if the window is modal to its parent
/// - `video.prop_window_create_mouse_grabbed_boolean`: true if the window starts with grabbed mouse focus
/// - `video.prop_window_create_open_gl_boolean`: true if the window will be used with OpenGL rendering
/// - `video.prop_window_create_parent_pointer`: an video.Window that will be the parent of this window, required for windows with the "tooltip", "menu", and "modal" properties
/// - `video.prop_window_create_resizable_boolean`: true if the window should be resizable
/// - `video.prop_window_create_title_string`: the title of the window, in UTF-8 encoding
/// - `video.prop_window_create_transparent_boolean`: true if the window show transparent in the areas with alpha of 0
/// - `video.prop_window_create_tooltip_boolean`: true if the window is a tooltip
/// - `video.prop_window_create_utility_boolean`: true if the window is a utility window, not showing in the task bar and window list
/// - `video.prop_window_create_vulkan_boolean`: true if the window will be used with Vulkan rendering
/// - `video.prop_window_create_width_number`: the width of the window
/// - `video.prop_window_create_x_number`: the x position of the window, or `SDL_WINDOWPOS_CENTERED (C macro)`, defaults to `SDL_WINDOWPOS_UNDEFINED (C macro)`. This is relative to the parent for windows with the "tooltip" or "menu" property set.
/// - `video.prop_window_create_y_number`: the y position of the window, or `SDL_WINDOWPOS_CENTERED (C macro)`, defaults to `SDL_WINDOWPOS_UNDEFINED (C macro)`. This is relative to the parent for windows with the "tooltip" or "menu" property set.
/// These are additional supported properties on macOS:
/// - `video.prop_window_create_cocoa_window_pointer`: the `(__unsafe_unretained)` NSWindow associated with the window, if you want to wrap an existing window.
/// - `video.prop_window_create_cocoa_view_pointer`: the `(__unsafe_unretained)` NSView associated with the window, defaults to `[window contentView]`
/// These are additional supported properties on iOS, tvOS, and visionOS:
/// - `video.prop_window_create_windowscene_pointer`: the `(__unsafe_unretained)` UIWindowScene associated with the window, defaults to the active window scene.
/// These are additional supported properties on Wayland:
/// - `video.prop_window_create_wayland_surface_role_custom_boolean` - true if the application wants to use the Wayland surface for a custom role and does not want it attached to an XDG toplevel window. See [README-wayland](README-wayland) for more information on using custom surfaces.
/// - `video.prop_window_create_wayland_create_egl_window_boolean` - true if the application wants an associated `wl_egl_window` object to be created and attached to the window, even if the window does not have the OpenGL property or `video.WindowFlags.open_gl` flag set.
/// - `video.prop_window_create_wayland_wl_surface_pointer` - the wl_surface associated with the window, if you want to wrap an existing window. See [README-wayland](README-wayland) for more information.
/// These are additional supported properties on Windows:
/// - `video.prop_window_create_win32_hwnd_pointer`: the HWND associated with the window, if you want to wrap an existing window.
/// - `video.prop_window_create_win32_pixel_format_hwnd_pointer`: optional, another window to share pixel format with, useful for OpenGL windows
/// These are additional supported properties with X11:
/// - `video.prop_window_create_x11_window_number`: the X11 Window associated with the window, if you want to wrap an existing window.
/// The window is implicitly shown if the "hidden" property is not set.
/// These are additional supported properties with Emscripten:
/// - `video.prop_window_create_emscripten_canvas_id_string`: the id given to the canvas element. This should start with a '#' sign
/// - `video.prop_window_create_emscripten_keyboard_element_string`: override the binding element for keyboard inputs for this canvas. The variable can be one of:
/// - "#window": the javascript window object (default)
/// - "#document": the javascript document object
/// - "#screen": the javascript window.screen object
/// - "#canvas": the WebGL canvas element
/// - "#none": Don't bind anything at all
/// - any other string without a leading # sign applies to the element on the page with that ID. Windows with the "tooltip" and "menu" properties are popup windows and have the behaviors and guidelines outlined in video.createPopupWindow().
/// If this window is being created to be used with an render.Renderer, you should not add a graphics API specific property (`video.prop_window_create_open_gl_boolean`, etc), as SDL will handle that internally when it chooses a renderer. However, SDL might need to recreate your window at that point, which may cause the window to appear briefly, and then flicker as it is recreated. The correct approach to this is to create the window with the `video.prop_window_create_hidden_boolean` property set to true, then create the renderer, then show the window with video.Window.show().
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `props`: the properties to use.
///
/// - **Returns:** the window that was created or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should only be called on the main thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** properties.create
/// - **See also:** video.createWindow
/// - **See also:** video.Window.deinit
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn createWindowWithProperties(props: PropertiesId) Error!Window {
    const result = c.SDL_CreateWindowWithProperties(props);
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return Window{ .value = @ptrCast(result.?) };
}

/// Return whether the cursor is currently being shown.
///
/// - **Returns:** `true` if the cursor is being shown, or `false` if the cursor is hidden.
