///   - `texture`: a texture with more than 1 mip level.
///
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
inline fn generateMipmapsForGpuTexture(command_buffer: ?GpuCommandBuffer, texture: ?GpuTexture) void {
    c.SDL_GenerateMipmapsForGPUTexture(if (command_buffer) |resource| @ptrCast(resource.value) else null, if (texture) |resource| @ptrCast(resource.value) else null);
}

/// Get metadata about your app.
///
/// This returns metadata previously set using setAppMetadata() or setAppMetadataProperty(). See setAppMetadataProperty() for the list of available properties and their meanings.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `name`: the name of the metadata property to get.
///
/// - **Returns:** the current value of the metadata property, or the default if it is not set, NULL for properties with no default.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread, although the string returned is not protected and could potentially be freed if you call setAppMetadataProperty() to set that property from another thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** setAppMetadata
/// - **See also:** setAppMetadataProperty
pub inline fn getAppMetadataProperty(name: ?[:0]const u8) ?[:0]const u8 {
    const result = c.SDL_GetAppMetadataProperty(if (name != null) @ptrCast(name.?.ptr) else null);
    return if (result == null) null else std.mem.span(@as([*:0]const u8, @ptrCast(result.?)));
}

/// Get the current assertion handler.
///
/// This returns the function pointer that is called when an assertion is triggered. This is either the value last passed to assert.setAssertionHandler(), or if no application-specified function is set, is equivalent to calling assert.getDefaultAssertionHandler().
/// The parameter `puserdata` is a pointer to a void*, which will store the "userdata" pointer that was passed to assert.setAssertionHandler(). This value will always be NULL for the default handler. If you don't care about this data, it is safe to pass a NULL pointer to this function to ignore it.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `puserdata`: pointer which is filled with the "userdata" pointer that was passed to assert.setAssertionHandler().
///
/// - **Returns:** the assert.AssertionHandler that is called when an assert triggers.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** assert.setAssertionHandler
inline fn getAssertionHandler(puserdata: ?*?*anyopaque) AssertionHandler {
    const result = c.SDL_GetAssertionHandler(@ptrCast(puserdata));
    return if (result == null) null else @ptrCast(result);
}

/// Get a list of all assertion failures.
///
/// This function gets all assertions triggered since the last call to assert.resetAssertionReport(), or the start of the program.
/// The proper way to examine this data looks something like this:
/// ```c
/// constSDL_AssertData*item=SDL_GetAssertionReport();
/// while(item){
/// printf("'%s',%s(%s:%d),triggered%utimes,alwaysignore:%s.\\n",
/// item->condition,item->function,item->filename,
/// item->linenum,item->trigger_count,
/// item->always_ignore?"yes":"no");
/// item=item->next;
/// }
/// ```
///
/// - **Returns:** a list of all failed assertions or NULL if the list is empty. This memory should not be modified or freed by the application. This pointer remains valid until the next call to quit() or assert.resetAssertionReport().
/// - **Thread safety:** This function is not thread safe. Other threads calling assert.resetAssertionReport() simultaneously, may render the returned pointer invalid.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** assert.resetAssertionReport
inline fn getAssertionReport() ?*const AssertData {
    const result = c.SDL_GetAssertionReport();
    return if (result == null) null else @ptrCast(result);
}

/// Named output values.
const GetAsyncIoResultResult = struct {
    /// Output `outcome`.
    outcome: AsyncIoOutcome,
};

/// Query an async I/O task queue for completed tasks.
///
/// If a task assigned to this queue has finished, this will return true and fill in `outcome` with the details of the task. If no task in the queue has finished, this function will return false. This function does not block.
/// If a task has completed, this function will free its resources and the task pointer will no longer be valid. The task will be removed from the queue.
/// It is safe for multiple threads to call this function on the same queue at once; a completed task will only go to one of the threads.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `queue`: the async I/O task queue to query.
///
/// - **Returns:** true if a task has completed, false otherwise.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** asyncIo.Queue.waitResult
/// Returns named output values.
inline fn getAsyncIoResult(queue: ?AsyncIoQueue) ?GetAsyncIoResultResult {
    var outcome_raw: @typeInfo(@typeInfo(@TypeOf(c.SDL_GetAsyncIOResult)).@"fn".params[1].type.?).pointer.child = undefined;
    if (!c.SDL_GetAsyncIOResult(if (queue) |resource| @ptrCast(resource.value) else null, &outcome_raw)) return null;
    return GetAsyncIoResultResult{
        .outcome = @bitCast(outcome_raw),
    };
}

/// Use this function to get the size of the data stream in an asyncIo.AsyncIo.
///
/// This call is *not* asynchronous; it assumes that obtaining this info is a non-blocking operation in most reasonable cases.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `asyncio`: the asyncIo.AsyncIo to get the size of the data stream from.
///
/// - **Returns:** the size of the data stream in the ioStream.IoStream on success or a negative error code on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn getAsyncIoSize(asyncio: ?AsyncIo) Error!i64 {
    const result = c.SDL_GetAsyncIOSize(if (asyncio) |resource| @ptrCast(resource.value) else null);
    if (result < 0) return error.SdlFailure;
    return result;
}

/// Get the value of an atomic variable.
///
/// ***Note: If you don't know what this function is for, you shouldn't use it!***
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `a`: a pointer to an atomic.Int variable.
///
/// - **Returns:** the current value of an atomic variable.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** atomic.setInt
inline fn getAtomicInt(a: ?*AtomicInt) c_int {
    return c.SDL_GetAtomicInt(@ptrCast(a));
}

/// Get the value of a pointer atomically.
///
/// ***Note: If you don't know what this function is for, you shouldn't use it!***
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `a`: a pointer to a pointer.
///
/// - **Returns:** the current value of a pointer.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** atomic.compareAndSwapPointer
/// - **See also:** atomic.setPointer
inline fn getAtomicPointer(a: ?*?*anyopaque) ?*anyopaque {
    const result = c.SDL_GetAtomicPointer(@ptrCast(a));
    return if (result == null) null else @ptrCast(result);
}

/// Get the value of an atomic variable.
///
/// ***Note: If you don't know what this function is for, you shouldn't use it!***
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `a`: a pointer to an atomic.U32 variable.
///
/// - **Returns:** the current value of an atomic variable.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** atomic.setU32
inline fn getAtomicU32(a: ?*AtomicU32) u32 {
    return c.SDL_GetAtomicU32(@ptrCast(a));
}

/// Get the current channel map of an audio device.
///
/// Channel maps are optional; most things do not need them, instead passing data in the [order that SDL expects](CategoryAudio#channel-layouts).
/// Audio devices usually have no remapping applied. This is represented by returning NULL, and does not signify an error.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `devid`: the instance ID of the device to query.
///
/// - **Returns:** an array of the current channel mapping, with as many elements as the current output spec's channels, or NULL if default. The returned slice is allocated with the caller-provided allocator.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** audio.Stream.setInputChannelMap
inline fn getAudioDeviceChannelMap(allocator_: std.mem.Allocator, devid: AudioDeviceId) Error![]c_int {
    const Count = @typeInfo(@typeInfo(@TypeOf(c.SDL_GetAudioDeviceChannelMap)).@"fn".params[1].type.?).pointer.child;
    var count: Count = 0;
    const result = c.SDL_GetAudioDeviceChannelMap(devid, &count);
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    defer c.SDL_free(@ptrCast(result));
    const length = std.math.cast(usize, count) orelse return error.SdlFailure;
    const copy = allocator_.alloc(c_int, length) catch return error.OutOfMemory;
    errdefer allocator_.free(copy);
    @memcpy(copy, result[0..length]);
    return copy;
}

/// Named output values.
const GetAudioDeviceFormatResult = struct {
    /// Output `spec`.
    spec: AudioSpec,
};

/// Get the current audio format of a specific audio device.
///
/// For an opened device, this will report the format the device is currently using. If the device isn't yet opened, this will report the device's preferred format (or a reasonable default if this can't be determined).
/// You may also specify SDL_AUDIO_DEVICE_DEFAULT_PLAYBACK (C macro) or SDL_AUDIO_DEVICE_DEFAULT_RECORDING (C macro) here, which is useful for getting a reasonable recommendation before opening the system-recommended default device.
/// You can also use this to request the current device buffer size. This is specified in sample frames and represents the amount of data SDL will feed to the physical hardware in each chunk. This can be converted to milliseconds of audio with the following equation:
/// `ms = (int) ((((Sint64) frames) * 1000) / spec.freq);`
/// Buffer size is only important if you need low-level control over the audio playback timing. Most apps do not need this.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `devid`: the instance ID of the device to query.
///   - `sample_frames`: pointer to store device buffer size, in sample frames. Can be NULL.
///
/// - **Returns:** true on success or false on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// Returns named output values.
inline fn getAudioDeviceFormat(devid: AudioDeviceId, sample_frames: ?*c_int) Error!GetAudioDeviceFormatResult {
    var spec_raw: @typeInfo(@typeInfo(@TypeOf(c.SDL_GetAudioDeviceFormat)).@"fn".params[1].type.?).pointer.child = undefined;
    if (!c.SDL_GetAudioDeviceFormat(devid, &spec_raw, @ptrCast(sample_frames))) return error.SdlFailure;
    return GetAudioDeviceFormatResult{
        .spec = @bitCast(spec_raw),
    };
}

/// Get the gain of an audio device.
///
/// The gain of a device is its volume; a larger gain means a louder output, with a gain of zero being silence.
/// Audio devices default to a gain of 1.0f (no change in output).
/// Physical devices may not have their gain changed, only logical devices, and this function will always return -1.0f when used on physical devices.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `devid`: the audio device to query.
///
/// - **Returns:** the gain of the device or -1.0f on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** audio.setDeviceGain
inline fn getAudioDeviceGain(devid: AudioDeviceId) f32 {
    return c.SDL_GetAudioDeviceGain(devid);
}

/// Get the human-readable name of a specific audio device.
///
/// **WARNING**: this function will work with SDL_AUDIO_DEVICE_DEFAULT_PLAYBACK (C macro) and SDL_AUDIO_DEVICE_DEFAULT_RECORDING (C macro), returning the current default physical devices' names. However, as the default device may change at any time, it is likely better to show a generic name to the user, like "System
/// default audio device" or perhaps "default [currently %s]". Do not store this name to disk to reidentify the device in a later run of the program, as the default might change in general, and the string will be the name of a specific device and not the abstract system default.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `devid`: the instance ID of the device to query.
///
/// - **Returns:** the name of the audio device, or NULL on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** audio.getPlaybackDevices
/// - **See also:** audio.getRecordingDevices
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn getAudioDeviceName(devid: AudioDeviceId) Error![:0]const u8 {
    const result = c.SDL_GetAudioDeviceName(devid);
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    return std.mem.span(@as([*:0]const u8, @ptrCast(result.?)));
}

/// Use this function to get the name of a built in audio driver.
///
/// The list of audio drivers is given in the order that they are normally initialized by default; the drivers that seem more reasonable to choose first (as far as the SDL developers believe) are earlier in the list.
/// The names of drivers are all simple, low-ASCII identifiers, like "alsa", "coreaudio" or "wasapi". These never have Unicode characters, and are not meant to be proper names.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `index`: the index of the audio driver; the value ranges from 0 to audio.getNumDrivers() - 1.
///
/// - **Returns:** the name of the audio driver at the requested index, or NULL if an invalid index was specified.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** audio.getNumDrivers
inline fn getAudioDriver(index: c_int) ?[:0]const u8 {
    const result = c.SDL_GetAudioDriver(index);
    return if (result == null) null else std.mem.span(@as([*:0]const u8, @ptrCast(result.?)));
}

/// Get the human readable name of an audio format.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `format`: the audio format to query.
///
/// - **Returns:** the human readable name of the specified audio format or "SDL_AUDIO_UNKNOWN" if the format isn't recognized.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
inline fn getAudioFormatName(format: AudioFormat) ?[:0]const u8 {
    const result = c.SDL_GetAudioFormatName(@intCast(@intFromEnum(format)));
    return if (result == null) null else std.mem.span(@as([*:0]const u8, @ptrCast(result.?)));
}

/// Get a list of currently-connected audio playback devices.
///
/// This returns of list of available devices that play sound, perhaps to speakers or headphones ("playback" devices). If you want devices that record audio, like a microphone ("recording" devices), use audio.getRecordingDevices() instead.
/// This only returns a list of physical devices; it will not have any device IDs returned by audio.openDevice().
/// If this function returns NULL, to signify an error, `*count` will be set to zero.
///
/// - **Returns:** a 0 terminated array of device instance IDs or NULL on error; call error_.get() for more information. The returned slice is allocated with the caller-provided allocator.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** audio.openDevice
/// - **See also:** audio.getRecordingDevices
inline fn getAudioPlaybackDevices(allocator_: std.mem.Allocator) Error![]AudioDeviceId {
    const Count = @typeInfo(@typeInfo(@TypeOf(c.SDL_GetAudioPlaybackDevices)).@"fn".params[0].type.?).pointer.child;
    var count: Count = 0;
    const result = c.SDL_GetAudioPlaybackDevices(&count);
    if (result == null) return error.SdlFailure;
    defer c.SDL_free(@ptrCast(result));
    const length = std.math.cast(usize, count) orelse return error.SdlFailure;
    const copy = allocator_.alloc(AudioDeviceId, length) catch return error.OutOfMemory;
    errdefer allocator_.free(copy);
    @memcpy(copy, result[0..length]);
    return copy;
}

/// Get a list of currently-connected audio recording devices.
///
/// This returns of list of available devices that record audio, like a microphone ("recording" devices). If you want devices that play sound, perhaps to speakers or headphones ("playback" devices), use audio.getPlaybackDevices() instead.
