/// Convert an integer into a string.
///
/// This requires a radix to specified for string format. Specifying 10 produces a decimal number, 16 hexadecimal, etc. Must be in the range of 2 to 36.
/// Note that this function will overflow a buffer if `str` is not large enough to hold the output! It may be safer to use stdinc.snprintf to clamp output, or stdinc.asprintf to allocate a buffer. Otherwise, it doesn't hurt to allocate much more space than you expect to use (and don't forget possible negative signs, null terminator bytes, etc).
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `value`: the integer to convert.
///   - `str`: the buffer to write the string into.
///   - `radix`: the radix to use for string generation.
///
/// - **Returns:** `str`.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** stdinc.uitoa
/// - **See also:** stdinc.ltoa
/// - **See also:** stdinc.lltoa
inline fn itoa(value: c_int, str: ?[*]u8, radix: c_int) ?[*]u8 {
    const result = c.SDL_itoa(value, @ptrCast(str), radix);
    return if (result == null) null else @ptrCast(result);
}

/// Get the status of a specified joystick.
