/// Request that the window be made as large as possible.
///
/// Non-resizable windows can't be maximized. The window must have the video.WindowFlags.resizable flag set, or this will have no effect.
/// On some windowing systems this request is asynchronous and the new window state may not have have been applied immediately upon the return of this function. If an immediate change is required, call video.Window.sync() to block until the changes have taken effect.
/// When the window state changes, an events.EventType.window_maximized event will be emitted. Note that, as this is just a request, the windowing system can deny the state change.
/// When maximizing a window, whether the constraints set via video.Window.setMaximumSize() are honored depends on the policy of the window manager. Win32 and macOS enforce the constraints when maximizing, while X11 and Wayland window managers may vary.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `window`: the window to maximize.
///
/// - **Returns:** true on success or false on failure; call error_.get() for more information.
/// - **Thread safety:** This function should only be called on the main thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** video.Window.minimize
/// - **See also:** video.Window.restore
/// - **See also:** video.Window.sync
///
/// Returns `error.SdlFailure` when SDL reports failure.
inline fn maximizeWindow(window: ?Window) Error!void {
    if (!c.SDL_MaximizeWindow(if (window) |resource| @ptrCast(resource.value) else null)) return error.SdlFailure;
}

/// Compare two buffers of memory.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `s1`: the first buffer to compare. NULL is not permitted!
///   - `s2`: the second buffer to compare. NULL is not permitted!
///
/// - **Returns:** less than zero if s1 is "less than" s2, greater than zero if s1 is "greater than" s2, and zero if the buffers match exactly for `len` bytes.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
inline fn memcmp(s1: []const u8, s2: []const u8) c_int {
    return c.SDL_memcmp(@ptrCast(s1.ptr), @ptrCast(s2.ptr), @intCast(if (s2.len == s1.len) s1.len else @panic("related slices must have equal lengths")));
}

/// Copy non-overlapping memory.
///
/// The memory regions must not overlap. If they do, use stdinc.memmove() instead.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `dst`: The destination memory region. Must not be NULL, and must not overlap with `src`.
///   - `src`: The source memory region. Must not be NULL, and must not overlap with `dst`.
///
/// - **Returns:** `dst`.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** stdinc.memmove
inline fn memcpy(dst: []u8, src: []const u8) *anyopaque {
    const result = c.SDL_memcpy(@ptrCast(dst.ptr), @ptrCast(src.ptr), @intCast(if (src.len == dst.len) dst.len else @panic("related slices must have equal lengths")));
    return @ptrCast(result.?);
}

/// Copy memory ranges that might overlap.
///
/// It is okay for the memory regions to overlap. If you are confident that the regions never overlap, using stdinc.memcpy() may improve performance.
///
/// - **Parameters:**
///   - `dst`: The destination memory region. Must not be NULL.
///   - `src`: The source memory region. Must not be NULL.
///
/// - **Returns:** `dst`.
/// - **Thread safety:** It is safe to call this function from any thread.
/// - **Since:** This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.
/// - **See also:** stdinc.memcpy
inline fn memmove(dst: []u8, src: []const u8) *anyopaque {
    const result = c.SDL_memmove(@ptrCast(dst.ptr), @ptrCast(src.ptr), @intCast(if (src.len == dst.len) dst.len else @panic("related slices must have equal lengths")));
    return @ptrCast(result.?);
}

/// Insert a memory acquire barrier (function version).
